Use the normal-equation representation \(J^TJs=-J^Tr\) when a positive-definite reduced system and an effective preconditioner for \(J^TJ\) are available and conditioning amplification is acceptable. Use the augmented system
\[
\begin{pmatrix}I_m&J\\J^T&0\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}y\\s\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}-r\\0\end{pmatrix}
\]
when avoiding explicit products, retaining the singular-value scale, or exploiting a symmetric-indefinite preconditioner is more important. The selected Krylov method must match the resulting operator class: positive definite for conjugate gradients, symmetric indefinite for methods supporting that class.

